 On apply, TA prints one `[applied]` or `[failed]` line per proposal. Proposals are replayed only when a person is present: from an interactive terminal, or when you pass `--replay-actions`. A non-interactive apply (a script, CI, the session auto-apply gate, the release pipeline) applies the files, prints how many external actions were not replayed, and leaves them for you to run with `ta draft apply <id> --replay-actions`. `--no-replay-actions`, or `TA_NO_REPLAY_ACTIONS=1`, turns replay off even in a terminal. `ta draft apply <id> --chain --dry-run` previews the whole chain and changes and replays nothing. A failed proposal never blocks the others or the file changes; fix the cause and re-run `ta draft apply`. Re-applying is safe: replayed proposals are recorded in `.ta/wayfinder-task-replay.log` and skipped next time, and created tasks always carry an `external_id` (derived from the goal and proposal ids when the agent did not supply one), so a task is never created twice. A project without `[plan] backend = "wayfinder"` simply skips the proposals with a log line. The Wayfinder service account needs only `member` role for these calls.
 
+#### Proposing Wayfinder Goals, KPI Metrics and Datapoints in a Draft
+
+Goals and KPIs follow the same rule as tasks: the Chief-of-Staff never writes to Wayfinder. A project-manager worker proposes them with four MCP tools, they ride in the same draft a person reviews, and they are sent to Wayfinder only when that draft is applied. None of these tools exist in a chat-mode session.
+
+| Tool | What it proposes | Applied as |
+|---|---|---|
+| `ta_propose_goal_create` | A goal: `name` and `kind` (a lowercase slug of at most 32 characters) required; `description`, `parent_goal_id`, `target_date` (`YYYY-MM-DD`) optional | `POST /api/projects/<project>/goals` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_metric_create` | A KPI metric under a goal: `goal_id`, `label`, `unit`, `target`, `cadence` (`none`, `daily`, `weekly`, `monthly`), `kind` (`task_count`, `external`), `aggregation` (`sum`, `latest`); `require_gate` and `weight` optional | `POST .../goals/<goal>/kpi-metrics` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_datapoint_add` | A datapoint: `metric_id`, `value`; `source` optional (default `manual`) | `POST .../kpi-metrics/<metric>/datapoints` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_create` | An org-level KPI: `label`; `description`, `weight`, `project_id` (omit for org-wide), `source_ref` optional | `POST /api/orgs/<org>/kpis` |
+
+Every call needs `goal_run_id`. Numbers (`target`, `weight`, `value`) must be finite and within +-1e12, enum fields must use the listed values, and text fields have length caps. A bad call is refused when the agent makes it, not at review time.
+
+**Referring to something proposed earlier.** Each tool returns a `ref` such as `ref:0`. A later proposal in the same draft can pass that string wherever it would pass a Wayfinder id (`parent_goal_id`, `goal_id`, `metric_id`). The reference must point at an earlier proposal of the right kind. At apply time it is replaced with the id Wayfinder returned for that step.
+
+```text
+ta_propose_goal_create(name="Grow signups", kind="objective", ...)        -> ref:0
+ta_propose_kpi_metric_create(goal_id="ref:0", kind="external", ...)       -> ref:1
+ta_propose_kpi_datapoint_add(metric_id="ref:1", value=42)                 -> ref:2
+```
+
+A `task_count` metric takes its progress from tasks, and Wayfinder refuses datapoints on it. Proposing a datapoint on such a metric created in the same draft is refused up front. For a metric that already exists, Wayfinder's refusal is reported at apply time as a conflict. Propose the metric as `external` when you need datapoints.
+
+**What the reviewer sees.** `ta draft view <draft-id>` lists each proposal under pending actions with what will be created, its `ref:<n>`, and the `external_id` it will be created under:
+
+```text
+  2. [pending] Propose KPI metric "Signups" (100 users, external, weekly) under goal ref:0 (state_changing)
+     KPI metric create (ref:1): Signups
+     Goal: ref:0
+     Target: 100 users (external, weekly, aggregation sum)
+     Weight: 1.0, require gate: false
+     Created on apply with external_id ta_draft:<draft-id>:1
+     Sent to Wayfinder only when this draft is approved and applied.
+```
+
+The `ref:<n>` number is the proposal's position in the draft counted from 0, while the list number counts from 1. Rejecting or marking a proposal for discussion keeps it out of apply, and anything that refers to it is reported as not attempted.
+
+**Apply behavior.** Proposals are sent in the order they were made, using the same replay rules as task proposals. Every create carries `external_id` `ta_draft:<draft-id>:<n>`, so Wayfinder treats a repeat as the same object. If a step fails, the steps that refer to it are not attempted and the message names which step they were waiting on. Independent steps still run. Finished steps and their returned ids are recorded in `.ta/wayfinder-task-replay.log`, so after fixing the cause you re-run `ta draft apply <draft-id>` and only the unfinished steps are sent. Error messages name the route, the status and what to do. A 403 names the missing right: `tasks:write` for goals, metrics and datapoints, `kpis:manage` for org KPIs. A 409 is reported as a conflict, meaning the same `external_id` was already used with different contents.
+
